{
  "dom_id": "question-133835",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14168483/assets/e5553b66879dcd4f8852f95d206e806b1ca378d8ca3e33cd9aeb3fc13bd22bed.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            When $x=12\\,\\text{m},$ the dimensions of Tom's plot of land are $11\\,\\text{m}$ and $14\\text{m}.$ A water pipe goes diagonally from one corner to the opposite, as shown above. Find the **exact** length of the pipe.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The length of the pipe is \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \nmeters.",
  "worked_solution": "First, notice that we have a right triangle with legs $a=11\\,\\text{m}$ and $b=14\\,\\text{m}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14168483/assets/87aee6e1c4071aa7efe82c19ffc6803f8f95a1b4e9108e0844b00593bc37434e.png)\n\nLet $c$ be the length of the hypotenuse. Then, according to the Pythagorean theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}^{2} & ={a}^{2}+{b}^{2} \\\\ c & =\\sqrt{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the values for $a$ and $b$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\sqrt{{11}^{2}+{14}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{121+196} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{317}\\,\\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
